POSTS["founder-led-content-d2c-playbook"] = "\n".join([
    tldr("Founder-led content is the highest-leverage channel a premium D2C brand has. " + link("LinkedIn B2B Institute", S["linkedin_b2b"]) + " research applies: 95% of the audience is not in-market. Founder posts compound trust across that 95%. " + link("Tinuiti", S["tinuiti"]) + " and " + link("Common Thread Collective", S["common_thread"]) + " both highlight founder visibility as a leading indicator of brand CAC improvement. The playbook below covers cadence (3 to 4 posts per week), format mix (POV, story, build-in-public, customer), and what to avoid."),
    p("Customers buy premium from people, not faceless catalogues. The fastest growing premium brands in 2024 to 2025 had visible founders. Not personality-driven, but visible. The playbook below covers what to post, where, and how often without turning the founder into a content factory."),
    p("Founder content fails most often for one reason: the founder tries to be the typist. The role is wrong. The founder is the source; a content lead is the typist. The structure below makes the program survive past month three."),
    h2("Why does founder content work better than brand content for premium D2C?"),
    cap("Founder content earns trust at 3 to 5x the rate of brand content because audiences attribute taste and judgment to people, not logos. A premium brand is a taste judgment delivered consistently."),
    p("Brand accounts post product. Founders post point of view. The point of view is what audiences associate with quality choices. A founder who shares the why behind a fabric, an ingredient, or a manufacturing decision earns more trust than a perfect product photograph ever will."),
    p("There is a second-order benefit. Founder content gets cited inside LLM responses for category queries far more often than brand content. " + link("Profound research", S["profound"]) + " on LLM citations shows people-attributed content over-indexes in retrieval. The " + ilink("get cited by ChatGPT piece", "get-cited-by-chatgpt-d2c-best-of") + " covers this in depth."),
    h2("What is the right cadence?"),
    cap("Three to four posts per week is the right cadence for most founders. Two main platforms (LinkedIn plus Instagram or TikTok), one supporting (the third). More burns the founder; less loses momentum."),
    ol([
        "LinkedIn. 2 long posts per week. Industry POV plus founder story.",
        "Instagram. 1 to 2 stories per day, 2 posts per week. Visual brand and product.",
        "TikTok. 2 to 3 short videos per week if the audience is there. Talking head plus product.",
        "Newsletter. Monthly long-form deep dive. Most underused channel.",
        "Podcast. Monthly guest appearances. Borrow other audiences.",
        "Quarterly long-form piece on the company blog. The piece this newsletter and podcasts cross-reference.",
    ]),
    h2("What format mix should the founder run?"),
    cap("Five formats balance well: POV piece, build-in-public, customer story, behind-the-scenes, and contrarian take. Rotate weekly, do not run the same format twice in a row."),
    ul([
        "POV piece. 'Here is what most brands get wrong about X.' Direct, specific, defensible.",
        "Build-in-public. A milestone, a decision, a number. Quietly, not braggy.",
        "Customer story. With permission, with detail. The buyer reads themselves into it.",
        "Behind-the-scenes. The supplier visit, the lab, the warehouse, the bad day.",
        "Contrarian take. Earn attention with a defensible disagreement.",
        "Curated reading. What the founder is reading and why; signals taste.",
    ]),
    h2("What should the founder avoid posting?"),
    cap("Avoid AI-generated copy that sounds like everyone else, humblebrag updates, vague industry commentary, and engagement-bait questions. Premium audiences spot performance fast."),
    ul([
        "AI slop. Audiences recognise it. Trust drops.",
        "Humblebrag. Specific numbers fine, fake humility burns.",
        "Vague industry commentary. Take a side or skip the post.",
        "Engagement bait. The metric goes up; the audience quality goes down.",
        "Daily posting at all costs. Burnout shows. Quality drops.",
        "Vague corporate speak. Founders post like people, not press releases.",
    ]),
    h2("How do you build a founder content engine that survives a year?"),
    cap("Two principles: a content lead translates founder thinking into drafts, and the founder approves and posts personally. The founder is the source, not the typist."),
    p("Most failed founder programs put the founder in the typist seat. The role is wrong. The founder talks, the content lead drafts, the founder edits and posts. Recording weekly 20-minute calls with the founder produces enough source material for 4 posts a week."),
    p("Tools to make this work: a transcription pipeline (Otter, Whisper), a shared Notion or Linear board for drafts, a brand-voice rule book, and a banned-phrase list. The content lead reuses the same draft frames so the founder's posting time stays at 15 minutes per piece."),
    h2("How do you measure founder content?"),
    cap("Measure with three signals: self-reported attribution at checkout (founder name mentioned), branded search lift, and inbound demand quality (LTV of customers acquired via founder-content months)."),
    ul([
        "Self-reported attribution. 'How did you first hear about us?' often surfaces the founder name.",
        "Branded search lift. The slow-burn truth of compounding founder content.",
        "Inbound demand quality. LTV of customers from founder months versus baseline.",
        "Profile views and follows on LinkedIn or Instagram. Leading indicator.",
        "Newsletter open rate and replies. A direct quality signal.",
    ]),
    faq([
        ("What if I am not a natural writer?", "You do not need to be. Voice notes plus a content lead works."),
        ("How fast does it start working?", "Visible inbound demand in 60 to 90 days. Compounding past 6 months."),
        ("Should I post on weekends?", "LinkedIn no, Instagram yes, TikTok yes."),
        ("Do I need a personal brand strategy?", "Light one. The strategy is the brand strategy. The founder is the medium."),
        ("Will it eat my week?", "Three to five hours per week, with a content lead. More without one."),
        ("Should the same person also lead the SaaS-style version of this?", "See " + ilink("founder thought leadership as a posting system", "founder-thought-leadership-saas-pipeline-system") + "; the rules are 80% the same."),
        ("Does it hurt to step back?", "Yes. Two weeks dark is recoverable; eight weeks is not."),
    ]),
    cta("Subscribe for the weekly founder content drop", "/#contact"),
    related("founder-led-content-d2c-playbook"),
])

POSTS["wholesale-retail-d2c-when-to-open"] = "\n".join([
    tldr("Wholesale and retail can either build or break a premium D2C brand. The decision is structural, not opportunistic. " + link("Common Thread", S["common_thread"]) + " shows omnichannel DTC brands hit 30 to 45% wholesale share at maturity. " + link("Shopify Research", S["shopify_research"]) + " confirms premium D2C brands that opened curated retail in the right year compound faster than pure-D2C peers. The framework below covers when to open, what to negotiate, and how to protect margin and brand position."),
    p("Premium D2C founders ask this question every year: should we open wholesale? The answer is yes, sometimes, with conditions. Below is the decision framework, the trade-offs, and the negotiations that make wholesale a performance channel rather than a margin tax."),
    p("The brands that get this right treat wholesale as a curated channel with its own brand book, not as a quick distribution add-on. The ones that get it wrong sign with anyone who will take them and watch the brand position dilute over 18 months."),
    h2("When should a premium D2C brand open wholesale?"),
    cap("Open wholesale when the D2C engine is profitable and stable, the brand has unmet demand in regions or formats, and a wholesale partner can reach buyers cheaper than paid media can."),
    ul([
        "D2C profitability is stable, not aspirational.",
        "There is genuine demand the website cannot serve (geography, gifting, discovery).",
        "Margin on wholesale lands above 35% gross with terms.",
        "The right partner is available without a damaging exclusive.",
        "The brand can hold price across channels.",
        "Operations can hit the retailer's SLA (especially returns and replenishment).",
    ]),
    h2("When should a premium D2C brand NOT open wholesale?"),
    cap("Skip wholesale when D2C unit economics are still being figured out, when wholesale partners would undercut your own pricing, or when production cannot meet retail SLAs without breaking."),
    ol([
        "D2C unit economics not solved. Solve there first.",
        "Margin too thin. Wholesale at sub-30% gross is a tax, not a channel.",
        "Production not ready. Retailers will return product if SLAs slip.",
        "Brand still finding voice. Retail freezes the brand in place; do that after, not during, positioning.",
        "Wholesale partner not aligned. The wrong shelf damages perception faster than no shelf.",
        "Founder not present. Retail relationships need senior attention; cannot delegate fully.",
    ]),
    h2("What should you negotiate with a wholesale partner?"),
    cap("Five non-negotiables: margin structure, payment terms, MAP compliance, marketing co-investment, and a clean exit clause. Ignore any of these and the deal compounds against you."),
    ul([
        "Margin. 45 to 60% wholesale margin to retailer is standard for premium.",
        "Terms. 30 to 60 day payment terms; not consignment for premium D2C.",
        "MAP compliance. Minimum advertised price enforced.",
        "Co-marketing. Brand storefront, training, in-store events.",
        "Exit clause. 12 to 18 month review with mutual termination.",
        "Data sharing. Sell-through data shared weekly; not quarterly black boxes.",
        "Return policy. Capped returns or non-returnable for specific SKUs.",
    ]),
    h2("How do you measure wholesale as a performance channel?"),
    cap("Track three things: sell-through rate at retail, brand search lift in the partner's regions, and incremental D2C revenue after the partnership launches."),
    p("Sell-through at the retailer is the partner's metric. Brand search lift in the partner's regions tells you whether retail is feeding D2C. Incremental D2C revenue (compared to a non-retail region as a control) is the most honest read."),
    p("Quarterly review meetings with the retailer should cover all three. Without the data exchange in the contract, the retailer has incentive to share only the flattering numbers. The exchange agreement is part of the negotiation, not an afterthought."),
    h2("How do you protect the brand inside wholesale?"),
    cap("Protect the brand by curating which retailers, controlling visual merchandising, and refusing the discount cycle. The brand says no to the wrong retailers, not yes to all."),
    ul([
        "Curate ruthlessly. Three good retailers beat ten mediocre ones.",
        "Brand merchandising playbook. Mandatory for partners. Auditable.",
        "No participation in retailer-led sales below 15% off without prior consent.",
        "Annual brand review meeting with each retailer.",
        "Walk if the partner refuses MAP or merchandising standards.",
        "Exclusive lines for retailers that earn them; not for all.",
    ]),
    h2("What is the cost of running wholesale internally?"),
    cap("Wholesale needs at minimum a wholesale lead, an operations partner, and finance support. Headcount typically rises 2 to 3 FTE in the first 12 months at premium scale."),
    p("Most founders underestimate the internal cost. Wholesale is not a 'set it up and forget' channel. Annual buying cycles, returns processing, in-store training, retailer-specific marketing, customs paperwork for international retailers, all add up. The 2 to 3 FTE is the floor, not the ceiling."),
    faq([
        ("Is Amazon wholesale?", "Treat Amazon as its own channel with its own playbook. Different game."),
        ("How do I price for wholesale?", "Wholesale should not undercut D2C on the same SKU. Use exclusive lines or formats if pressure builds."),
        ("Should I start with one retailer or many?", "One. Learn the playbook. Replicate."),
        ("What about pop-up retail?", "Excellent test before permanent wholesale. Lower risk, real signal."),
        ("Will wholesale cannibalise D2C?", "Some, in shared regions. The math should add up before signing."),
        ("How does this fit with international expansion?", "Wholesale is often the first international move. Less ops risk than direct."),
        ("What if the retailer wants a discount-led launch?", "Walk. The launch sets the perception."),
    ]),
    cta("Book a wholesale readiness call", "/#contact"),
    related("wholesale-retail-d2c-when-to-open"),
])

